Follow the pipeline in [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md) — design before code, and stop and ask if a
request contradicts a documented convention.

Before editing anything here, read:

- [docs/conventions/30-api.md](../../docs/conventions/30-api.md) for routes, controllers, the
  `{ isOk, status, message, data }` envelope, `runListQuery`, auth and validation
- [docs/conventions/20-schema.md](../../docs/conventions/20-schema.md) for models, indexes,
  backfills, soft delete and the audit trail
- [docs/conventions/60-limits.md](../../docs/conventions/60-limits.md) before promising anything —
  it is the list of what this starter deliberately does not do

The four that are most often got wrong:

- **Guard every route, twice.** `authMiddleware(roles)` for the session, then
  `checkPermission(menuUrl, action)` for the permission matrix on anything belonging to an admin
  screen. The matrix is enforced server-side but only on routes that ask for it — a route without
  it is open to any logged-in user. `menuUrl` must match a seeded `MenuMaster.menuUrl` exactly; a
  typo fails closed for every non-admin and looks fine to you, because ADMIN bypasses. The only
  unauthenticated routes in the repo are the public SEO ones, and adding another has a
  four-question gate in 30-api.md.
- **Every list endpoint goes through `runListQuery`.** Its `filterable` map is an allowlist and a
  security boundary — never build a match from `req.body` directly, and never widen it to "whatever
  the model has".
- **`filterable` on the server and `filterFields` in `apps/admin/src/entities/` describe the same
  set.** Change both together, and index every field in it.
- **Declare indexes on every `ref`, every filterable field, and every business-unique key.** Most
  existing models don't; that's the gap, not the pattern.

Two global Mongoose plugins govern every model, including one you add today:

- `models/softDelete.js` — nothing is ever removed; deletes set `isDeleted` and every read hides it.
- `models/auditPlugin.js` — every write made inside a logged-in request is recorded to `AuditLog`.
  Writes with no session user (the seeder, public endpoints, counters) are deliberately not.

You write no code for either. Both must be imported before any model — entry points do it on their
first two imports, and both throw on boot rather than silently skipping a model.
